Based on my thorough exploration of the andesim repository, here is a detailed report of how the hybrid driver picks QEMU options to match the vsim engine:

## 1. Engine Descriptor Parsing (`--describe` output)

**File:** `/home/nick/work/andesim/driver/engine_descriptor.hpp` (lines 25-50)

```cpp
struct EngineDescriptor {
  std::string id;                       // "qemu", "verilator:ax45mpv_premium"
  std::string type;                     // qemu | verilator | alioth | sid
  std::string kind;                     // functional | cycle-accurate
  long vlen = 0;                        // RVV params; 0 = not reported
  long dlen = 0;
  long elen = 0;
  long xlen = 0;
  std::vector<std::string> triggers;    // marker | pc
  std::vector<std::string> roles;       // standalone | fast-leg | accurate-leg
  std::vector<std::string> state;       // resume | drain (hybrid_state_v1 caps)
  std::vector<std::string> andes_csrs;  // Andes CSRs this engine implements
  std::vector<EngineInvoke> invoke;     // argv template per role
  std::string result;                   // stderr:<re> | state.mcycle
  std::string bin_path;                 // resolved path to the binary
  std::string source;                   // built-in | --describe | sidecar
};
```

The descriptor is populated from JSON via `descriptor_from_json()` (engine_descriptor.hpp:54-83). VSim engines are discovered by running `sim_<core> --describe` and parsing JSON output, or falling back to a `<bin>.json` sidecar file.

**Discovery Function:** `/home/nick/work/andesim/driver/engine_registry.cpp` lines 104-143 (`describe_one()` function)

---

## 2. QEMU CPU Selection (No Per-Engine Map Currently Exists)

**Key Finding:** The driver does NOT have an automatic engine-to-QEMU-CPU mapping. The fast-leg ALWAYS uses a hardcoded QEMU CPU string.

**File:** `/home/nick/work/andesim/driver/engine_registry.cpp` lines 30-79 (`builtin_qemu_arch()`)

